 \begin{abstract}
-What do recovered transformations reveal about language-model representations beyond prediction? We distinguish fitted prediction, minimum risk over an exact-action class, and preference for a prescribed semantic action. Under a common second-moment-whitened metric, a spectral budget respecting real irreducible types bounds the optimal prediction risk of fixed-dimensional exact orthogonal actions within a factor of two. An analytical example shows that optimal unconstrained regression risk and ordinary orbit spectra do not determine this risk. We study weekday cycles and square-corner actions in six frozen Llama and Qwen language models. An independent orthogonal comparator separates orthogonality cost from bounds on the further cost of common group laws. At dimension 16, the latter lower endpoints exceed the fixed numerical tolerance in 46 of 48 conditions, but their magnitudes are heterogeneous: the weekday median is $5.35\times10^{-6}$, and one condition contributes $99.44\%$ of the weekday lower-endpoint sum. These are bounds, not measured exact costs or statistical significance claims. Constructed exact actions transfer less accurately than independent least squares; exhaustive small-group image comparisons give condition- and dimension-dependent spectral-budget rankings of the prescribed registration. A separate new-context study finds that difference fitting improves weekday transport relative to coordinate fitting but worsens corner transport. The results connect a computable action-class target to representation measurements while separating compatibility from semantic preference and native computation.
+A map that predicts changes in a language model's representations need not explain which transformation structure the model supports. We study this distinction for prescribed finite-group actions on visible input states, using one operator family across contexts in fixed pretrained coordinates. Our theory connects a real-type-constrained spectral budget to the minimum prediction risk over fixed-dimensional exact orthogonal actions. Under a common second-moment-whitened loss, it bounds that class optimum within a factor of two; an analytical example shows why unconstrained regression risk and ordinary orbit spectra alone are insufficient. In six frozen Llama and Qwen models, weekday and square-corner transformations are predictively recoverable, while finite-bank bounds quantify the additional cost of requiring an exact orthogonal action. An independent orthogonal comparison separates orthogonality cost from bounds on the further cost of common group laws. The latter are highly heterogeneous: at dimension 16, one condition contributes $99.44\%$ of the weekday lower-bound sum. Constructed exact actions predict held-out representations less accurately than independent least squares. Exhaustive small-group registration comparisons show that the prescribed semantic image is not uniformly favored, and its spectral ranking changes with retained dimension. A separate authored-context study finds opposite transport effects across the two domains. These results quantify exact-action compatibility while distinguishing it from predictive performance, semantic preference and native computation.
 \end{abstract}
 
