+\paragraph{Recovering transformations in representations.}
+Paired-map fitting asks whether a change in inputs can be described by a map between their representations; linear and affine maps have also been used for network stitching \citep{lenc2015understanding}. Sun studies conditions for transformations to descend through an encoder, the difference between shared and local realizations, and errors under the induced representation metric \citep{sun2026transformationlaws}. Homomorphism errors provide a further diagnostic of compositional generalization \citep{an2026homomorphism}. These works motivate our observation model, but a fitted map's error is not the optimum over every exact orthogonal action of a fixed dimension. That latter quantity is our theoretical target. Equivariance by Contrast addresses a different identification problem: it jointly learns coordinates and actions under assumptions on mixing, data diversity and conditional-distribution matching \citep{schmidt2025equivariance}. Here the pretrained coordinates are fixed and the labeled input action is supplied. We neither discover that action without supervision nor extend the guarantees for jointly learned coordinates.
 
